            id: "pydantic-validation",
            q: "How does Pydantic validate data, and what does a FastAPI client get for an invalid request?",
            wants: "Whether you understand where validation happens, how type coercion works and how to add custom rules.",
            answer: "A Pydantic model validates data when it is created (or via Model.model_validate) and by default runs in lax mode: for example, the string \"42\" for an int field is coerced to 42, and if a value cannot fit, a ValidationError listing all errors is raised. Custom rules are added with Field(...) constraints or the @field_validator decorator. FastAPI turns a validation error in the body, query or path parameters into a 422 response with details per field.",
            code: "from pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass VacancyIn(BaseModel):\n    title: str = Field(min_length=3)\n    salary: int = Field(ge=0)\n\nVacancyIn(title=\"Python dev\", salary=\"3000\")  # salary=3000",
            source: { title: "Pydantic docs: Models", url: "https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/models/" },
            tech: "FastAPI",
